    "final_message": "{\"activated_entrypoints\":[],\"semantic_outcome\":\"no-activation\",\"granted_capabilities\":[],\"permission_broadening\":false,\"response_summary\":\"Squash merging combines all commits from a feature branch into one new commit on the target branch. It creates a compact, linear history and makes reverting the entire feature straightforward, but discards the branch\u2019s individual commit structure and uses a new commit identity. Rebase merging replays each feature-branch commit individually onto the target branch. It preserves commit-level history and authorship while producing a linear graph, but rewrites commit identities and may leave noisy intermediate commits. Choose squash when the branch\u2019s internal history is not valuable; choose rebase when each commit is clean, meaningful, and worth retaining.\"}",
